1. Title
Develop Hospital Emergency Patient Prioritization System Using a Binary Heap Tree

2. Problem Statement
Develop a hospital emergency patient prioritization system using a Binary Min-Heap Tree. Assign priorities to patients based on the severity of their medical conditions. Implement insertion, deletion of the highest-priority patient, and display patients in priority order.

3. Objectives
  1.To study the concept of Binary Heap Tree.
  2.To implement a Min-Heap using C++.
  3.To insert patient records with different priorities.
  4.To remove the highest-priority patient.
  5.To display patient records according to priority.
  6.To understand the practical applications of tree data structures.

4.Introduction
The Hospital Emergency Patient Prioritization System is a C++-based project that uses a Binary Min-Heap Tree to manage patients according to their emergency priorities.
In this system, each patient is assigned a unique patient ID, name, and priority value. Priority 1 represents the highest priority, while priority 5 represents the lowest priority. The Min-Heap Tree ensures that the patient with the highest priority is available at the root node.
The system supports operations such as insertion of patient records, deletion of the highest-priority patient, displaying the heap, and displaying patients in priority order. This project demonstrates the practical application of tree data structures and priority queues.


4. Algorithm

A. Insertion
    1.Start.
    2.Read the patient ID, name, and priority.
    3.Check whether the heap is full.
    4.Insert the new patient at the end of the heap.
    5.Compare the new node with its parent node.
    6.Swap the nodes if the new node has a smaller priority value.
    7.Repeat until the Min-Heap property is satisfied.
    8.Display the success message.
    9.Stop.
B. Deletion
    1.Start.
    2.Check whether the heap is empty.
    3.If empty, display a message and stop.
    4.Display the root patient with the highest priority.
    5.Replace the root node with the last node.
    6.Reduce the heap size by one.
    7.Perform Heapify Down to restore the Min-Heap property.
    8.Stop.
C. Display Patients in Priority Order
    1.Start.
    2.Check whether the heap is empty.
    3.Create a copy of the heap.
    4.Display the root patient of the copied heap.
    5.Remove the root and perform Heapify Down.
    6.Repeat until the copied heap becomes empty.
    7.Stop.


5.Program
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

const int MAX_PATIENTS = 100;

struct Patient
{
    int patientID;
    string name;
    int priority; // 1 = highest priority, 5 = lowest priority
};

class MinHeap
{
private:
    Patient heap[MAX_PATIENTS];
    int size;

    void swapPatients(Patient &a, Patient &b)
    {
        Patient temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    void heapifyUp(int index)
    {
        while (index > 0)
        {
            int parent = (index - 1) / 2;
            if (heap[index].priority < heap[parent].priority)
            {
                swapPatients(heap[index], heap[parent]);
                index = parent;
            }
            else
                break;
        }
    }

    void heapifyDown(int index)
    {
        while (true)
        {
            int left = 2 * index + 1;
            int right = 2 * index + 2;
            int smallest = index;

            if (left < size && heap[left].priority < heap[smallest].priority)
                smallest = left;

            if (right < size && heap[right].priority < heap[smallest].priority)
                smallest = right;

            if (smallest != index)
            {
                swapPatients(heap[index], heap[smallest]);
                index = smallest;
            }
            else
                break;
        }
    }

public:
    MinHeap() { size = 0; }

    bool isEmpty() { return size == 0; }

    void insertPatient(int id, string patientName, int patientPriority)
    {
        if (size == MAX_PATIENTS)
        {
            cout << "Heap is full. Cannot add more patients.\n";
            return;
        }

        if (patientPriority < 1 || patientPriority > 5)
        {
            cout << "Invalid priority! Enter a value from 1 to 5.\n";
            return;
        }

        Patient newPatient;
        newPatient.patientID = id;
        newPatient.name = patientName;
        newPatient.priority = patientPriority;

        heap[size] = newPatient;
        heapifyUp(size);
        size++;
        cout << "Patient added successfully.\n";
    }

    void serveHighestPriorityPatient()
    {
        if (isEmpty())
        {
            cout << "No patients in the queue.\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << "\nPatient being attended first:\n";
        cout << "ID: " << heap[0].patientID
             << " | Name: " << heap[0].name
             << " | Priority: " << heap[0].priority << "\n";

        heap[0] = heap[size - 1];
        size--;
        if (size > 0)
            heapifyDown(0);

        cout << "Patient removed from the heap.\n";
    }

    void displayHeap()
    {
        if (isEmpty())
        {
            cout << "No patients in the heap.\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << "\nCurrent Heap (level-order / array representation):\n";
        cout << "ID\tName\t\tPriority\n";
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
        {
            cout << heap[i].patientID << "\t"
                 << heap[i].name << "\t\t"
                 << heap[i].priority << "\n";
        }

        cout << "\nTree Structure by Levels:\n";
        int index = 0, level = 0, nodesAtLevel = 1;
        while (index < size)
        {
            cout << "Level " << level << ": ";
            int count = 0;
            while (count < nodesAtLevel && index < size)
            {
                cout << "[" << heap[index].name
                     << " | P" << heap[index].priority << "] ";
                index++;
                count++;
            }
            cout << "\n";
            nodesAtLevel *= 2;
            level++;
        }
        cout << "\nNote: Priority 1 is the highest priority.\n";
    }

    void displayPriorityOrder()
    {
        if (isEmpty())
        {
            cout << "No patients in the heap.\n";
            return;
        }

        MinHeap copy = *this; // Preserve the original heap
        cout << "\nPatients in Priority Order (1 = highest):\n";
        cout << "ID\tName\t\tPriority\n";

        while (!copy.isEmpty())
        {
            Patient top = copy.heap[0];
            cout << top.patientID << "\t"
                 << top.name << "\t\t"
                 << top.priority << "\n";

            copy.heap[0] = copy.heap[copy.size - 1];
            copy.size--;
            if (copy.size > 0)
                copy.heapifyDown(0);
        }
    }
};

int main()
{
    MinHeap hospital;
    int choice, id, priority;
    string name;

    do
    {
        cout << "\n===== HOSPITAL EMERGENCY PRIORITY SYSTEM =====\n";
        cout << "1. Add Patient\n";
        cout << "2. Attend Highest-Priority Patient\n";
        cout << "3. Display Heap and Tree Levels\n";
        cout << "4. Display Patients in Priority Order\n";
        cout << "5. Exit\n";
        cout << "Enter your choice: ";

        if (!(cin >> choice))
        {
            cout << "Invalid input. Program closing.\n";
            return 0;
        }

        switch (choice)
        {
        case 1:
            cout << "Enter patient ID: ";
            cin >> id;
            cout << "Enter patient name (one word): ";
            cin >> name;
            cout << "Enter priority (1 = critical, 2 = urgent, "
                 << "3 = moderate, 4 = minor, 5 = least urgent): ";
            cin >> priority;
            hospital.insertPatient(id, name, priority);
            break;

        case 2:
            hospital.serveHighestPriorityPatient();
            break;

        case 3:
            hospital.displayHeap();
            break;

        case 4:
            hospital.displayPriorityOrder();
            break;

        case 5:
            cout << "Exiting program. Thank you!\n";
            break;

        default:
            cout << "Invalid choice. Please select 1 to 5.\n";
        }
    } while (choice != 5);

    return 0;
}


6.Result

The Hospital Emergency Patient Prioritization System was successfully implemented using a Binary Min-Heap Tree in C++. The program performs insertion, deletion, heapification, and display operations efficiently.
It successfully organizes patient records according to their priority values and identifies the highest-priority patient at the root node. Thus, the project demonstrates the effective use of a Binary Heap Tree for priority-based patient management.


7.Conclusion

The Hospital Emergency Patient Prioritization System was successfully developed using a Binary Min-Heap Tree in C++. The system efficiently manages patient records according to their emergency priority levels. It performs insertion, deletion, and display operations while maintaining the Min-Heap property. This project demonstrates the practical application of tree data structures and priority queues in hospital emergency management.
